WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:03.840
- [Instructor] We're told that
Chloe takes a slice of pizza

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.700
out of the freezer and leaves
it on the counter to defrost.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.819
She models the relationship

00:00:09.819 --> 00:00:12.980
between the temperature P of the pizza.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:14.430
This seems like it's
going to be interesting.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:18.312
The temperature P of the
pizza in degrees Celsius,

00:00:18.312 --> 00:00:23.312
and time T since she took it
out of the freezer in minutes.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.200
As P is equal to 20 minus 25 times 0.8

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.720
to the T power.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:36.430
So that's how she's modeling
her temperature of the pizza.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:39.960
P as a function of time.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.040
She wants to graph the relationship

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.110
over the first 25 minutes.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:47.291
So what we're going to do
here is not so much focus

00:00:47.291 --> 00:00:48.780
on the graph itself

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.640
although we will look at that.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.550
I'm actually just going to
use a graphing calculator

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.200
in order to have access to the graph.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.403
But I wanna look at the
graph in the context

00:00:57.403 --> 00:00:59.760
of what we are trying to model

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.870
and carefully think about
what should be the labels

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:04.320
for the axes,

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.073
what parts of the graph are interesting?

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:09.103
So this is right

00:01:09.103 --> 00:01:12.570
over here is this function
graphed on Desmos.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.570
You can see I typed it in right over here.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:15.403
P is equal

00:01:15.403 --> 00:01:19.300
to 20 minus 25 times 0.8 to the T power.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.521
Exactly what we had down here.

00:01:22.521 --> 00:01:25.950
Now remember, this is
modeling the temperature

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:29.300
of our pizza as a function of time.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:33.550
So to help us remember that,
let's put in some labels

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.280
for our axes.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.720
So to graph, settings.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.340
If I go down here, our x-axis.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:43.980
Now our x-axis is really the T-axis.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.360
That's our independent variable over here.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.183
And what is it measuring?

00:01:49.183 --> 00:01:51.080
Well it says it right over here.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.064
It's measuring time T in minutes.

00:01:54.064 --> 00:01:55.970
So we could write it like this.

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:59.973
T, which is measured in minutes.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.942
And then, what about our y-axis?

00:02:03.942 --> 00:02:07.510
Well this is really our P-axis

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.580
and that's measuring degrees Celsius.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.055
So that's our P-axis,

00:02:12.055 --> 00:02:16.330
and it's measuring degrees Celsius.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:22.393
All right.

00:02:22.393 --> 00:02:25.140
So let's just look at what
our graph looks like so far.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.100
So there we have it.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.650
We've put in our axes and we
have already typed this part

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.810
in so I can focus on the graph itself.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:32.890
Now are we done?

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:35.570
Is this all we need to really think about?

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:38.050
Well the next part to
think about is the domain

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:40.150
and what part of the y-axis.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.870
So what part of the range
are we really interested in?

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:46.440
Well the first thing to realize
is we're modeling something

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:47.910
as a function of time.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:51.740
And so we really shouldn't
be having negative time here.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.260
And we wanna think about the relationship

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.570
over the first 25 minutes.

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:59.910
So let's go back here.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:03.108
And when we look at the range of x values

00:03:03.108 --> 00:03:04.180
that we care about,

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.408
and really that we could think
about the part of the domain

00:03:06.408 --> 00:03:08.030
that we care about,

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.917
we wanna restrict to x being greater than

00:03:09.917 --> 00:03:11.690
or equal to zero.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:13.480
And obviously in this situation,

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.410
x is really T and then we
can also think about it

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:17.380
as less than 25.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.150
We don't have to restrict the upper bound,

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:23.020
but these are the first 25
minutes that she cares about.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.620
So let's do it like that.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:28.330
And now let's look at our graph.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:29.680
And the important things

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.610
to appreciate is that we have the axes.

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:35.130
We can see them.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:38.240
And so at time T is equal to zero,

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.700
we see that we actually
have a negative temperature

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.470
in degrees Celsius, and that makes sense.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:43.570
It came out of the freezer

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:45.510
so it's below freezing.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:47.608
And then we see that the pizza is warming

00:03:47.608 --> 00:03:51.130
up as it gets closer and
closer to room temperature,

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:51.963
which over here,

00:03:51.963 --> 00:03:55.520
looks like it's pretty
close to 20 degrees Celsius.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.180
And so now it looks like
we have been able to graph

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.650
what Chloe is trying to look at.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.210
It looks like we have modeled it well,

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.050
we have labeled it accordingly,

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:09.240
and we have set the ranges
of x values and the ranges

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.220
of y values that we'd wanna look at.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:14.415
The y values we just wanna
make sure that over the range

00:04:14.415 --> 00:04:16.280
of x values, and it's really a subset

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:18.740
of the domain not to confuse
the term range too much.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:22.230
The subset of the domain of the
x values that we care about,

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.680
that we can see the
corresponding y values.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:27.250
And we very clearly can see them.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:28.940
And we're essentially done.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:30.370
We've thought about how to best look

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:31.720
at the graph of this model.

